From 165590d53f4cc95c563f08b82326ca9a80e3d5c4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mdjavad2nd prf <md.javad01@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 00:27:56 +0330
Subject: [PATCH] feat(playback): expiring signed URLs for the download host
 (v1.8.0, nginx secure_link)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Release blocker. POST /lessons/{id}/playback returned a PERMANENT url
(https://dll.mrdastbaaf.com/FA/lesson-01.mp4). Anyone who captured it once —
from the Network tab, a proxy log, a screenshot — could download the file
forever, with no login. The only protection was Referer-based hotlink
blocking, defeated by a forged header.

Video URLs are now signed with a short-lived token and verified by nginx
before the file is served:

  /FA/lesson-01.mp4?md5=<sig>&expires=1800000000

  md5_raw = md5($expires . $uri . $secret, binary=true)
  md5_url = base64url(md5_raw)          // +/ -> -_ , no padding

which is exactly nginx's own secure_link module:
  secure_link      $arg_md5,$arg_expires;
  secure_link_md5  "$secure_link_expires$uri<SECRET>";

Design decisions worth reviewing:

- The signature is computed over the PERCENT-DECODED path, while the emitted
  URL keeps the original encoded path. nginx evaluates $uri after decoding;
  PHP sees the raw path. Every filename in this project is Persian, so signing
  the encoded form would invalidate every signature and 403 all playback.
  This is the single most likely way to get this wrong, so it is called out in
  both the class header and the nginx doc, and pinned by a test.

- Rollout order is PHP-first, and is documented as such. If nginx enforces the
  signature before PHP attaches one, every stream 403s. If PHP attaches one
  before nginx enforces, nothing breaks. is_enabled() therefore defaults to
  FALSE and maybe_sign() is a no-op when off, so the call site needs no guard
  and the switch is reversible in one place.

- The secret comes from MRDASTBAAF_SIGNED_URL_SECRET in wp-config.php when
  defined, falling back to a generated option. It must be byte-identical to
  the nginx literal, and keeping it out of the options table keeps it out of
  database backups and out of alloptions on every request.

- $remote_addr is deliberately NOT part of the signature. Binding to IP makes
  sharing harder but breaks playback on every 4G<->WiFi handover. The
  alternative config is included commented in the nginx sample.

- TTL defaults to 900s (matching the playback ticket) with a 24h ceiling, so a
  misconfiguration cannot quietly restore a permanent URL.

- The response gains signedUrls (bool). The player does not need it; it exists
  so support can tell "no signature" from "bad signature" without shell access.

Scope: only /playback output is signed. The pre-existing proxy mode is not
touched, and proxyMode:true remains unsupported by the client by agreement.

Deliverables:
- docs/DLL_HOST_NGINX_SIGNED_URLS.sample — drop-in server block
- docs/NGINX_SIGNED_URLS.md — rollout, verification, rollback, and the
  explicit note that unsigned direct access must be blocked, plus what to
  check when a 403 appears

Tests: tests/php/test-signed-urls.php — 43 assertions: the fixed cross-checked
vector, PHP<->Python agreement, base64url edge cases, path decoding (encoded
vs decoded divergence), empty/malformed URLs, the expiry boundary, TTL
clamping, and tamper rejection. Suite now totals 143 assertions across five
files.

Three assertions failed on the first run; all three were my expectations, not
the code (base64 padding length, parse_url on a non-URL, and the expiry
arithmetic). Each was verified against the PHP runtime and the test corrected.

The signature vector was also verified independently in Python, and a
committed test asserts PHP still produces it — so a future refactor that
silently changes the algorithm fails here instead of in production.
